    ],
    "historical_context": "Bartók composed this suite in 1915 during his intensive fieldwork collecting folk melodies across Transylvania (then part of Hungary), transforming seven authentic peasant tunes — reduced to six in the piano version — into a compact concert piece that strips away Romantic ornamentation in favor of modal austerity and rhythmic precision. The work became one of his most widely performed compositions, existing in versions for piano solo, small orchestra (1917), and numerous arrangements, and stands as a landmark in the early-20th-century effort to integrate genuine folk idiom into the Western art-music tradition.",
    "imslp_url": "https://imslp.org/wiki/Romanian_Folk_Dances,_Sz.56_(Bart%C3%B3k,_B%C3%A9la)",
